an be eaten plain or with coconut shavings. The taste is slightly sweet with a nutty flavor. It is a popular seasonal dessert across Vietnam, especially in Red River Delta cuisine. It is traditionally produced at the Cốm Vòng village in Hanoi."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-com"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%E1%BB%91m"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Vietnamese cuisine","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Viet World Kitchen","ref":"site/vietworldkitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"vietworldkitchen.com","note":"Andrea Nguyen's blog on Vietnamese cooking, ingredients and technique.","url":"https://vietworldkitchen.com/blog/category/recipes-vietnamese","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Hungry Huy","ref":"site/hungryhuy-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"hungryhuy.com","note":"Vietnamese-American Huy Vu's recipes for home-style Vietnamese dishes.","url":"https://www.hungryhuy.com/vietnamese/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Vietnamese cuisine","links":[["Alkemē","dish/alkeme"],["Bánh bao","dish/banh-bao"],["Bánh bột lọc","dish/banh-bot-loc"],["Bánh giầy","dish/banh-giay"],["Bánh tráng","dish/banh-trang"],["Chả rươi","dish/cha-ruoi"],["Chả trứng","dish/cha-trung"],["Chạo tôm","dish/chao-tom"],["Coconut candy","dish/coconut-candy"],["Ha VL","dish/ha-vl"],["Hanoi Kitchen","dish/hanoi-kitchen"],["Indochine (restaurant)","dish/indochine-restaurant"],["Khô cá lóc","dish/kho-ca-loc"],["Lee's Sandwiches","dish/lee-s-sandwiches"],["Lúc Lắc Vietnamese Kitchen","dish/luc-lac-vietnamese-kitchen"],["Mắm (restaurant)","dish/mam-restaurant"],["Mắm nêm","dish/mam-nem"],["Mama Đút","dish/mama-ut"],["Matta (restaurant)","dish/matta-restaurant"],["Nước chấm","dish/nuoc-cham"],["Ốc vú nàng","dish/oc-vu-nang"],["Phở Hòa","dish/pho-hoa"],["Phở Kim","dish/pho-kim"],["Pho Oregon","dish/pho-oregon"],["Rose VL Deli","dish/rose-vl-deli"],["Shaking beef","dish/shaking-beef"],["Tamarind Tree (restaurant)","dish/tamarind-tree-restaurant"],["The Black Sheep (restaurant)","dish/the-black-sheep-restaurant"],["Tương","dish/tuong"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5c/Com.JPG/500px-Com.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Bình Giang","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Com.JPG"}},"dish/grits":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/grits","n":"Grits","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Grits is a type of porridge made from coarsely ground dried corn (maize) or hominy, the latter being maize that has been treated with an alkali in a process called nixtamalization, with the pericarp (ovary wall) removed. It is eaten primarily in the United States. Grits are cooked in warm salted water or milk. They are often served with flavorings as a breakfast dish, though it is also served as a lunch or supper dish, particularly with meat.  Grits can be savory or sweet, with savory seasonings being more common. Grits are similar to other thick maize-based porridges from around the world, such as polenta, mieliepap, and mămăligă. Italian-Americans living in the delta of Arkansas and Mississippi recognized this and have substituted grits for traditional polenta dishes. The dish originated in the Southern United States but is now available nationwide. Grits are part of the dinner main di"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-grits"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grits"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Maize flour","ingredient/maize-flour"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["5-8 Club","dish/5-8-club"],["Altabira City Tavern","dish/altabira-city-tavern"],["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Applebee's","dish/applebee-s"],["Arby's","dish/arby-s"],["Arnold's Bar and Grill","dish/arnold-s-bar-and-grill"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Be Our Guest Restaurant","dish/be-our-guest-restaurant"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Ben's Chili Bowl","dish/ben-s-chili-bowl"],["Benjy's","dish/benjy-s"],["Besaw's","dish/besaw-s"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blackhawk (restaurant)","dish/blackhawk-restaurant"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Bobcat Bite","dish/bobcat-bite"],["Boiling Springs Tavern","dish/boiling-springs-tavern"],["Brix Tavern","dish/brix-tavern"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brown Derby","dish/brown-derby"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Buona","dish/buona"],["Busboys and Poets","dish/busboys-and-poets"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Byways Cafe","dish/byways-cafe"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["California Grill","dish/california-grill"],["Camp Washington Chili","dish/camp-washington-chili"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Carnation Cafe","dish/carnation-cafe"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/06/Grits1.jpg/500px-Grits1.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"sashafatcat","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grits1.jpg"}},"dish/melba-toast":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/melba-toast","n":"Melba toast","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from British cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Melba toast is a dry, crisp and thinly sliced rusk, often served with soup and salad or topped with either melted cheese or pâté. It is named after Dame Nellie Melba, the stage name of Australian opera singer Helen Porter Mitchell. Its name is thought to date from 1897, when the singer was very ill and it became a staple of her diet. The toast was created for her by a chef who was also a fan of hers, Auguste Escoffier, who also created the peach Melba dessert for her. The hotel proprietor César Ritz supposedly named it in a conversation with Escoffier."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-melba-toast"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melba_toast"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Toast","ingredient/toast"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Great British Chefs: traditional British recipes","ref":"site/greatbritishchefs-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"greatbritishchefs.com","note":"UK chef-driven recipe site with a large section of classic British dishes.","url":"https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/features/our-most-popular-traditional-british-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from British cuisine","links":[["68–86 Bar and Restaurant","dish/68-86-bar-and-restaurant"],["Banbury cake","dish/banbury-cake"],["Battenberg cake","dish/battenberg-cake"],["Black pudding","dish/black-pudding"],["Bread sauce","dish/bread-sauce"],["Carpetbag steak","dish/carpetbag-steak"],["Chelsea bun","dish/chelsea-bun"],["Christmas pudding","dish/christmas-pudding"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Crumble","dish/crumble"],["Crumpet","dish/crumpet"],["Curry in the United Kingdom","dish/curry-in-the-united-kingdom"],["Digestive biscuit","dish/digestive-biscuit"],["Dinner by Heston Blumenthal","dish/dinner-by-heston-blumenthal"],["Dundee cake","dish/dundee-cake"],["English muffin","dish/english-muffin"],["Flies' graveyard","dish/flies-graveyard"],["Flummery","dish/flummery"],["Fritter","dish/fritte